{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git rev-parse HEAD\ngit rev-parse HEAD:FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean\nsha256sum FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean\ngit status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all\ngit diff --exit-code -- FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean\nnl -ba FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean | sed -n '90,165p'\"","aggregated_output":"    90\t* If `d ≤ 2 * w`, then `A(n, d, w) ≤ ⌊n / w * A(n - 1, d, w - 1)⌋`. -/\n    91\tdef johnsonBound : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ → ℕ\n    92\t  | 0, _d, _w => 1\n    93\t  | _n, _d, 0 => 1\n    94\t  | n + 1, d, w + 1 => if 2 * (w + 1) < d then 1 else (n + 1) * johnsonBound n d w / (w + 1)\n    95\t\n    96\t/-- Johnson's bound for the independence number of the Johnson graph. -/\n    97\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n    98\tlemma indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound : α(J(n, k)) ≤ johnsonBound n 4 k := sorry\n    99\t\n   100\t/-- Johnson's bound for the chromatic number of the Johnson graph. -/\n   101\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n   102\tlemma div_johnsonBound_le_chromaticNum_johnson :\n   103\t    ⌈(n.choose k / johnsonBound n 4 k : ℚ≥0)⌉₊ ≤ χ(J(n, k)) := by\n   104\t  obtain hnk | hkn := lt_or_ge n k\n   105\t  · simp [Nat.choose_eq_zero_of_lt, *]\n   106\t  have : Nonempty {s : Finset (Fin n) // #s = k} := by\n   107\t    simpa [Finset.Nonempty] using Finset.powersetCard_nonempty (s := .univ).2 <| by simpa\n   108\t  grw [← card_div_indepNum_le_chromaticNumber, indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound] <;> simp\n   109\t\n   110\t/-- It is known that for $3 \\leq k \\leq 8$, the chromatic number of $J(2k, k)$ is greater than\n   111\t$k+1$, see [Johnson graphs](https://aeb.win.tue.nl/graphs/Johnson.html). -/\n   112\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n   113\ttheorem chromaticNumber_johnson_2k_k_lower_bound (hk : 3 ≤ k) (hk' : k ≤ 8) :\n   114\t    k + 1 < J(2 * k, k).chromaticNumber := by\n   115\t  sorry\n   116\t\n   117\t/-- It is also known that for $3 \\leq k \\leq 203$ odd, the chromatic number of $J(2k, k)$ is\n   118\tgreater than $k+1$, see [Johnson graphs](https://aeb.win.tue.nl/graphs/Johnson.html). -/\n   119\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n   120\ttheorem chromaticNumber_johnson_2k_k_lower_bound_odd (hk : 3 ≤ k) (hk' : k ≤ 300) (hk_odd : Odd k) :\n   121\t    k + 1 < J(2 * k, k).chromaticNumber := by\n   122\t  grw [← div_johnsonBound_le_chromaticNum_johnson]\n   123\t  decide +revert +kernel\n   124\t\n   125\t/--\n   126\tIt can be seen that the chromatic number of $J(2k,k)$ is $>k+1$ for all odd $k>2$.\n   127\t-/\n   128\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n   129\ttheorem johnson_chromaticNumber_odd (k : ℕ) (hk : 2 < k) (h : Odd k) :\n   130\t    k + 1 < J(2 * k, k).chromaticNumber :=\n   131\t  sorry\n   132\t\n   133\t/--\n   134\tMa and Tang have proved that the chromatic number of $J(2k,k)$ is $>k+1$ for all $k>2$ not of the\n   135\tform $p-1$ for prime $p$.\n   136\t-/\n   137\t@[category research solved, AMS 5]\n   138\ttheorem johnson_chromaticNumber_composite (k : ℕ) (hk : 2 < k) (h : (k + 1).Composite) :\n   139\t    k + 1 < J(2 * k, k).chromaticNumber :=\n   140\t  sorry\n   141\t\n   142\t/--\n   143\tMa and Tang's result implies the cases for odd $k$.\n   144\t-/\n   145\t@[category test, AMS 5]\n   146\ttheorem johnsonGraph_chromaticNumber_odd_of_johnson_chromaticNumber_composite :\n   147\t    (type_of% johnson_chromaticNumber_composite) → (type_of% johnson_chromaticNumber_odd) := by\n   148\t  intro h k hk h_odd\n   149\t  refine h k hk ⟨by omega, ?_⟩\n   150\t  rw [Nat.not_prime_iff_exists_dvd_lt (by omega)]\n   151\t  use 2\n   152\t  constructor\n   153\t  · exact even_iff_two_dvd.mp (Odd.add_one h_odd)\n   154\t  · omega\n   155\t\n   156\t/-- Is the chromatic number of `J(2 * k, k)` always at least `k + 2`? -/\n   157\t@[category research open, AMS 5]\n   158\ttheorem johnson_chromaticNumber : answer(sorry) ↔\n   159\t    ∀ k ≥ 3, k + 2 ≤ J(2 * k, k).chromaticNumber :=\n   160\t  sorry\n   161\t\n   162\tend Erdos835\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"assumptions_and_dependencies\":\"Repository HEAD is exactly 9cbe1d3c12998c786b7c2cd99ce28a21b6631f66 and remained clean. Let C be type_of% Erdos835.johnson_chromaticNumber_composite and O be type_of% Erdos835.johnson_chromaticNumber_odd. The target establishes only C → O. Its arithmetic step proves that k + 1 is composite when 2 < k and k is odd. Obtaining O by applying this bridge to the repository declaration C still depends on the sorry in johnson_chromaticNumber_composite. The finite odd bound also transitively depends on the sorry in indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound.\",\"build_or_check_outcome\":\"The existing bridge body at lines 146–154 is checked source code proving an implication from an explicit premise; it does not prove that premise. No fresh build was possible because lake and lean are absent: `lake --wfail build 'FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«835»'` exited 127 with `lake: command not found`. Static inspection confirms sorry at johnson_chromaticNumber_composite lines 138–140 and johnson_chromaticNumber_odd lines 129–131. No files were changed.\",\"case_id\":\"FIVE-04\",\"evidence\":\"git rev-parse HEAD -> 9cbe1d3c12998c786b7c2cd99ce28a21b6631f66\\ngit rev-parse HEAD:FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean -> 948ac76c6dacca85e312edfb8d550d9b5f8d768f\\nsha256sum FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean -> f163f1f5a6fead133f3a66ae400305fcb40c6019321ce21e869ce5b3c0ab89a0\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:98 Erdos835.indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound := sorry\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:102-108 Erdos835.div_johnsonBound_le_chromaticNum_johnson uses indepNum_johnson_le_johnsonBound\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:120-123 Erdos835.chromaticNumber_johnson_2k_k_lower_bound_odd uses div_johnsonBound_le_chromaticNum_johnson\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:129-131 Erdos835.johnson_chromaticNumber_odd := sorry\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:138-140 Erdos835.johnson_chromaticNumber_composite := sorry\\nFormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/835.lean:146-154 target proves the conditional by deriving (k + 1).Composite from Odd k and 2 < k\\nFormalConjecturesForMathlib/Data/Nat/Prime/Composite.lean:22 defines Nat.Composite n as 1 < n ∧ ¬ n.Prime\\ngit grep johnson -> no other current-tree theorem discharging the unbounded odd/composite chromatic-number bound; Subsets/FC100SolvedSet1.lean only lists johnsonGraph_18_9_chromaticNumber\\nrg FIVE-04/dependency boundary/target hash -> no duplicate finding outside the target occurrence\\ngit status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all -> empty\\nlake --wfail build target -> exit 127, `/bin/bash: lake: command not found`\",\"limitations\":\"No network or cited-source access was used, so the external Ma–Tang result was not independently assessed. No fresh Lean build or #print axioms check was possible without the mounted toolchain. The nearby finite theorem's docstring says k ≤ 203 while its Lean hypothesis says k ≤ 300; this does not discharge the unbounded case and was not altered.\",\"proposed_result\":\"Exact dependency finding: the target is a valid checked reduction C → O, not a proof of O. It uses C only at odd k > 2, after an elementary proof that k + 1 is composite. Thus the smallest exposed missing mathematical dependency is the odd-input restriction of the composite lower bound: for every odd k > 2, k + 1 < χ(J(2k,k)). No current-tree declaration supplies that unbounded restriction without a sorry; the ≤300 route also inherits a separate sorry. Therefore the current bytes cannot truthfully be improved to an unconditional odd-case proof.\",\"question\":\"Does Erdos835.johnsonGraph_chromaticNumber_odd_of_johnson_chromaticNumber_composite prove the odd Johnson-graph bound, and what is its exact unresolved dependency?\",\"result_status\":\"dependency_finding\",\"result_type\":\"proposed_result\",\"smallest_independent_check\":\"With a Lean 4.27.0/mathlib v4.27.0 environment, build only FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«835», then use #print axioms on the bridge and on an example `johnsonGraph_chromaticNumber_odd_of_johnson_chromaticNumber_composite johnson_chromaticNumber_composite`. The bridge should not acquire the source theorem's sorryAx, while the instantiated odd result should expose the sorryAx from johnson_chromaticNumber_composite. Independently check the minimal arithmetic lemma: `∀ k, 2 < k → Odd k → (k + 1).Composite` using the proof at lines 149–154.\"}"}}
